<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Taylor Series</title>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose you have this information about
        a function:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            f(0) = 2, \quad f'(0) = -3,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            f''(0) = -1, \quad f'''(0) = 0,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            f^{(4)}(0) = -3.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Form a Taylor polynomial for <m>f(x)</m>
        and use it to estimate <m>f(0.5)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="41">
      <p>
        The Taylor polynomial is
        <md>
          T(x) = 2 - 3x - \frac{x^2}{2}
          - \frac{x^4}{8}.
        </md>
        Evaluating at <m>x = 0.5</m> gives
        <md>
          T(0.5) = 2 - 1.5 - 0.125 - 0.0078
          \approx 0.367.
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt{1+x} =
        (1+x)^{1/2}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="4in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the first five terms of the
          Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="41">
        <p>
          Computing derivatives at <m>x = 0</m>, we see that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              f(x) \amp = (1+x)^{1/2}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f'(x) \amp = \frac12(1+x)^{-1/2}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f''(x) \amp = -\frac14(1+x)^{-3/2}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f'''(x) \amp = \frac38(1+x)^{-5/2}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f(x)^{(4)} \amp = -\frac{15}{16}(1+x)^{-7/2}
            </mrow>
          </md>
          This means that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              f(0) \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f'(0) \amp = \frac{1}{2}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f''(0) \amp = -\frac{1}{4} 
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f'''(0) \amp = \frac{3}{8}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f^{(4)}(0) \amp = -\frac{15}{16}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Therefore
          <md>
            f(x) \approx 1 + \frac{x}{2}
            - \frac{x^2}{8} + \frac{x^3}{16}
            - \frac{5x^4}{128}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the first five terms to approximate
          <m>\sqrt{1.1}</m> (what value of
          <m>x</m> should you use?) and compare
          your approximation to the exact value.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="41">
        <p>
          Use <m>x = 0.1</m> so that
          <md>
            \sqrt{1.1} \approx 1 + 0.05
            - 0.00125 + 0.0000625
            - 0.0000039
            \approx 1.04881.
          </md>
          The exact value is 
          <md>
            \sqrt{1.1} \approx 1.04881
          </md>
          so the error is less than
          <m>0.000001</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+x}} =
        (1+x)^{-1/2}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the first five terms of the
          Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="41">
        <p>
          Computing derivatives at <m>x = 0</m> gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              f(0) \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f'(0) \amp = -\frac{1}{2}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f''(0) \amp = \frac{3}{4}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f'''(0) \amp = -\frac{15}{8}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              f^{(4)}(0) \amp = \frac{105}{16}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Therefore
          <md>
            f(x) \approx 1 - \frac{x}{2}
            + \frac{3x^2}{8} - \frac{5x^3}{16}
            + \frac{35x^4}{128}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How can you use this to find the Taylor
          series for
          <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-u^2}}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="41">
        <p>
          Substitute <m>x = -u^2</m> so that
          <md>
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-u^2}} \approx
            1 + \frac{u^2}{2} +
            \frac{3u^4}{8} +
            \frac{5u^6}{16} +
            \frac{35u^8}{128}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          You may remember that
          <md>
            \arcsin(x) = \int_0^x
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-u^2}}\,du.
          </md>
          Use the first five terms of your
          Taylor series to estimate
          <m>\arcsin(0.1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="41">
        <p>
          Integrating term by term, we see that
          <md>
            \arcsin(x) \approx x +
            \frac{x^3}{6} +
            \frac{3x^5}{40} +
            \frac{5x^7}{112} +
            \frac{35x^9}{1152}.
          </md>
          At <m>x = 0.1</m>, we have
          <m>0.1 + 0.000167 + 0.0000008
          + \cdots \approx 0.100168</m>.
          The actual value is
          <m>\arcsin(0.1) \approx 0.100167</m>,
          so the error is less than
          <m>0.000001</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>f(x) = 3 + 4x - 7x^2</m>.
        Find the Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m>.
        What do you notice?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="41">
      <p>
        Since <m>f(0) = 3</m>,
        <m>f'(0) = 4</m>,
        <m>f''(0) = -14</m>, and
        <m>f^{(n)}(0) = 0</m> for <m>n \geq 3</m>,
        the Taylor series is
        <md>
          3 + 4x + \frac{-14}{2!}x^2
          = 3 + 4x - 7x^2.
        </md>
        The Taylor series of a polynomial is
        just the polynomial itself.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
